{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"LOOP ITERATION: stack is RED (1014/1023/1043/1048/1067 Verified -1). ROOT CAUSE is stack ORDERING, not any single change. Measured runner.py at each position: 7468=795, f184=802, dc0d=815, 2682=670, 2932=713, 9249=713, 281f=713, 3a98=680. Two distinct breaches: (a) f184 and dc0d exceed the 800-LOC HARD CAP because main's runner.py is now 798 and they ADD to it, while the split that reduces it (2682) sits 4th; (b) 2932/9249/281f sit at 713, over 2682's OWN attested <700 headroom test, which 3a98 only fixes at the top. Against the OLD main (~698) this ordering was fine — the 13-commit rebase onto a 798-line runner.py is what made the stack physically invalid mid-way. FIX: reorder so the two split changes precede the ones that grow runner.py: 7468 -> 2682 -> 3a98 -> f184 -> dc0d -> 2932 -> 9249 -> 281f. Projected per-position LOC: 795, 650, 617, 624, 637, 680, 680, 680 — every position under BOTH the 800 cap and the 700 target. Legitimate because 2682/3a98 are pure refactors of runner.py with no ticket dependency on f184/dc0d (2682 was discovered during S4 but does not depend on it). Expect conflicts where dc0d edits _pai_check_config, which 3a98 will have already moved to structured_run.py."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785458959865253001,"uuid":"6db835cf-1f09-41ae-9431-1beb7ceb099d"}